@meta
id: 01-14
title: Drill — five doors, one way back
chapter: 01
type: drill
skills: mode-awareness, esc, insert-i, insert-a, cmdline
par: 30
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: nothing — there is one room and no doors
open: batch.md

@brief
Eight times, the same loop. Step into each mode and come straight back out
with `Esc` before doing the actual edit:

    i  Esc    a  Esc    R  Esc    v  Esc    :  Esc

Then finish the line so it reads `status: queued`, and save and quit.

Five doors, one key that closes all of them. That is the whole idea of
this chapter and this is the last time you will be asked to practise it
on its own.

@keys
| `i` `a` | insert mode, left and right of the cursor |
| `R` | replace mode |
| `v` | visual mode |
| `:` | the command line |
| `Esc` | normal mode, from all five |

@hints
Say the mode out loud as you enter it for the first three reps, then stop
saying it and keep the rhythm. Do not check the indicator before pressing
`Esc`.
--
The edit itself is `$a` then ` queued`, `Esc`, `:wq`. It is the small part.

@solution
    i <Esc> a <Esc> R <Esc> v <Esc> : <Esc>
    $ a <space> queued <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Notice that `Esc` in the command line does not run the command, it
abandons it, and `Esc` in visual mode does not act on the selection, it
drops it. There is no door where `Esc` commits something by accident. That
is why it is safe to press it when you have no idea what state you are in,
and why experienced vim users press it constantly and without thinking.

If your little finger is complaining by rep eight, that is real and
common. `Ctrl-[` is the identical key without the reach, and a remap of
`jk` is the usual fix later. Get the reflex first; move the key second.

@start batch.md
Payout batch 07 status:

@expected batch.md
Payout batch 07 status: queued
